      "content": "right, i get that it's \"complex,\" but corporations spend billions on R&D and behavioral psychology every year. if they could find a way to do it with something even remotely subtle—like a low-level dopamine trigger that just makes you feel slightly more impulsive or \"reward-seeking\" in certain aisles—wouldn't the ROI be massive? i'm just trying to wrap my head around the technical hurdles. like, if a company wanted to implement a \"protocol\" like that, what would be the biggest mechanical or chemical obstacles they'd have to solve to make it undetectable to the average person?",
